ly wash a white sock with their darks sometimes find that sock comes out grey or blue, even when the other clothes look fine. The colour catcher sheets famously trap dyes of many colours — not just red. #### 4. A simple home test you can do As I mentioned before, next time you run a mixed dark load, throw in a small white cotton scrap (like an old handkerchief or white sock you’re happy to sacrifice). If there’s a non-colourfast item (a new red shirt, dark jeans, etc.), you’ll often see a faint tint on that white scrap — pink, blue, or grey — even though the other clothes look unchanged. That’s direct evidence that dye transfer happened everywhere, but only the white revealed it. --- ### But why does your personal experience still *feel* wrong? Because you’ve only ever seen the dramatic outcome (pink shirt) and never the hidden one. The mixed load comes out looking fine, so it’s natural to think “nothing happened.” But absence of visible evidence isn’t absence of the process itself. Think of it like this: if you drop a drop of red ink on a white sheet, you see a big red stain. If you drop the same drop on a dark blue sheet, you likely see nothing — but the ink is still there, just invisible against the dark background. You’d be right to say “ink ruined my white sheet,” but wrong to conclude that ink only damages white sheets. The same applies to dye in the washing machine. --- So you can believe my explanation not because I’m contradicting common sense, but because **I’m explaining *why* the common sense you already have is true** — and then offering the logical extension that dye transfer is universal, even when it’s invisible. That make sense now?
